WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:04.403
There are thousands of companies you could invest in.

2
00:00:04.403 --> 00:00:06.238
Picking winners is hard.

3
00:00:06.238 --> 00:00:08.684
What if you didn't have to pick?

4
00:00:09.585 --> 00:00:11.758
An index is just a list.

5
00:00:11.758 --> 00:00:17.824
The S and P 500 is a list of about five hundred large US companies.

6
00:00:18.724 --> 00:00:23.924
An index fund buys every company on that list automatically.

7
00:00:23.924 --> 00:00:24.964
No guessing.

8
00:00:25.864 --> 00:00:28.696
Bigger companies get a bigger slice.

9
00:00:28.696 --> 00:00:34.598
This is called being weighted by market cap, which just means company size.

10
00:00:35.498 --> 00:00:38.019
When you buy one share of the fund,

11
00:00:38.019 --> 00:00:42.267
you own a tiny piece of all five hundred companies at once.

12
00:00:43.168 --> 00:00:47.529
Because a computer just follows the list, costs are low.

13
00:00:47.529 --> 00:00:49.399
On ten thousand dollars,

14
00:00:49.399 --> 00:00:54.383
a zero point zero five percent fee is about five dollars a year.

15
00:00:54.383 --> 00:01:00.147
A typical actively managed fund might charge seventy-five dollars or more.

16
00:01:01.047 --> 00:01:06.309
The index has had scary drops, like in 2020 and 2022.

17
00:01:06.309 --> 00:01:11.174
But over long periods, it has historically grown.

18
00:01:11.174 --> 00:01:14.352
There are no guarantees, though.

19
00:01:15.251 --> 00:01:20.500
Own a little of everything, keep costs low, and stay patient.

20
00:01:20.500 --> 00:01:24.716
That's why so many people start with index funds.

21
00:01:24.716 --> 00:01:28.416
This is for learning, not financial advice.

